## 1. Introduction
Nitrate concentration in surface and ground water has dramatically increased during the last decades originated from excessive use of fertilizer, combustion of fossil fuels and discharge of ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ containing wastewater [1–4]. Nitrate pollution causes widespread eutrophication and threatens the health of human beings [5,6]. The in vivo conversion of ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ to $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ can lead to methemoglobinemia, even cancer [7]. Numerous techniques have been developed to remove nitrate from water, such as reverse osmosis, ion exchange, biological denitrification, catalytic and photocatalytic reduction [8–13]. However, these solutions have some limitations, including high cost, the need of secondary treatment and the sluggish kinetics. Electrocatalytic ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ reduction reaction $(\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{R R})$ utilizes electron as a green reductant to produce harmless $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ or more valueadded $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ , representing a powerful alternative to eliminate ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ from polluted water [14–16].
Ammonia is a versatile compound that can be used as fertilizer precursor, chemical feedstock and fuel. To date, it is challenging to achieve high-performance $\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{R R}$ catalysts, especially with high selectivity for ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ due to the complexity of reduction products. Recently, Cu-based electrocatalysts were found to exhibit excellent ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ activity owing to the appropriate electronic structure [17– 19]. Nevertheless, the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ selectivity is not satisfied, especially in the neutral $\mathsf{p H}$ and $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ is often produced during the $\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{R R}$ process, which should be avoided as $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ is more toxic than $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ [20–23]. In addition, Cu-based catalysts usually suffer from obvious deactivation due to surface passivation, metal leaching and particle aggregation [24–26].
New strategies should be developed to solve the above issues. Encapsulating and confining the metal catalysts in a porous framework is a promising way to achieve highly active, selective and stable $\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{R R}$ electrocatalysts [27–30]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed by uniformly distributed metal ions and organic ligands are ideal templates to fabricate carbon-supported metal catalysts [31,32]. It provides several advantages, including: (i) fully exposed active sites on evenly dispersed nanoparticles; (ii) favorable mass transfer with high porosity and large specific surface area; (iii) modulated electronic structure of catalysts through facile doping of heteroatoms; (iv) increased stability owing to the strong interaction between carbon support and metal nanoparticles. However, the reports referring to MOF derived ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}$ - RR electrocatalysts is still in its infancy.
Herein, we report a series of $\mathsf{C u N i}$ alloy electrocatalysts encapsulated in nitrogen-doped carbon (CuNi/NC) for highly active and selective $\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{R R}.$ The catalysts were prepared through direct pyrolysis of bimetallic CuNi-MOFs with varying Cu-Ni molar ratios. CuNi alloy nanoparticles are well-dispersed on the nitrogen-doped carbon support with hierarchically porous structure. The CuNi/NC catalysts exhibit excellent ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ performance with a nitrate conversion rate of $81.2\%$ and ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ selectivity of $94.4\%$ , greatly suppressing the production of toxic $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ . To understand the underlying mechanism, a combination of online differential electrochemical mass spectrometry (DEMS), in situ surface-enhanced infrared absorption spectroscopy (SEIRAS), and density functional theory (DFT) calculations were conducted to reveal the reaction mechanism and kinetics.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
Copper(II) nitrate trihydrate $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $299.0\%$ ), nickel(II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99.0\%$ , polyvinylpyrrolidone (MW: 40000, K30), N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.0\%$ , ethanol $(\geq99.7\%)$ , sodium nitrate $(\mathsf{N a N O}_{3}$ , $\geq98.0\%$ ), sodium phosphate monobasic dihydrate $\mathrm{\DeltaNaH_{2}P O_{4}{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ , $\geq99.0\%$ , sodium phosphate dibasic dodecahydrate $\mathrm{(Na_{2}H P O_{4}{\cdot}12H_{2}O}$ , $\geq99.0\%$ , , sulfuric acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}S0_{4},95-98\%),$ hydrogen peroxide $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0_{2}\geq30\%)$ , ammonium fluoride solution $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}F}$ $40\%$ ), hydrofluoric acid (HF, $48{-}51\%$ were obtained from the Sinopharm Group Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China. 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $_{\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}}$ , $299\%$ ) was supplied by the Shanghai Aladdin Bio-Chem Technology Co., LTD. Carbon black was purchased from Cabot Co., Ltd. Nafion D520 dispersion $(5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ was provided by Dupont. The carbon paper $(0.28\ \mathrm{mm}$ thickness) as the substrate of working electrode was obtained from Toray.
## 2.2. Preparation of CuNi/NC catalysts and the electrodes
Synthesis of MOF precursors. The CuNi-BTC and Ni-BTC precursors were synthesized according to a previous work with some modifications [33]. Typically, $7.2\ \mathrm{mmol}$ $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and Ni $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ with different molar ratios were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF (solution A). 4.8 mmol $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ and $0.43\mathrm{~g~}$ PVP was dissolved in another $10\mathrm{mL}$ DMF (solution B). Then, solution B was added into solution A with magnetic stirring for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Next, the mixture was transferred into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless autoclaves and kept at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ . After that, the precipitates with blue-green color were collected through centrifugation and washed with anhydrous ethanol for three times, followed drying at $60^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . For CuBTC, the synthetic conditions were the same except that the solvothermal temperature was lowered to $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to avoid the production of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ impurity.
Preparation of the working electrodes. The as-synthesized $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts $(7.5~\mathrm{mg})$ and carbon powder ( $\mathrm{:}1.5\mathrm{mg})$ were dispersed in a solution containing ethanol $(1.8~\mathrm{mL}$ ), isopropyl alcohol $\mathrm{^{\prime0.45\mL}}$ ) and $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ nafion $(22.5\upmu\mathrm{L})$ with ultrasonic treatment for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then the catalyst ink was dropped onto the two sides of one carbon paper with a size of $1.5\times1.5~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and then dried at room temperature.
## 2.3. Characterizations
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on the XRD6100 diffractometer (SHIMADZU, Japan) under a Cu Ka X-ray radiation. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) analysis and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy mapping was performed on Sig$\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ma300}}$ (ZEISS, Germany). Transmission electron microscopic (TEM) and Scanning transmission electron microscopic (STEM) analysis was performed on JEM-2100F (JEOL, Japan). Raman measurements were conducted on LabRAM HR Evolution spectrometer (HORIBA, Japan). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was carried out on ESCALAB 250Xi (Thermo Fisher Scientific, USA) under an Al $K\mathsf{a}$ X-ray irradiation. The bulk Cu-Ni ratio in CuNi/NC catalysts was determined by M4 Tornado X-ray fluorescence spectroscopy (Bruker, Germany). The nitrogen adsorption–desorption data were recorded on a BSD-PS2 Surface Area & Porosity Analyzer (Beishide Instrument, China) at 77 K, after degassing the samples at $473\ \mathrm{K}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The concentration of $N O_{3}^{-}{-}N$ $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ -N and $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ -N in the solution were analyzed by the gas-phase molecular absorption spectrograph (GMA 3370, Beiyu, China). The isotopic labeling experiments were conducted using ${\mathrm K}^{15}{\mathrm N}{\mathrm O}_{3}$ as the nitrogen source in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ phosphate buffer solution (PBS) and the product was determined by 1H NMR (Bruker Avance NEO 600).
## 2.4. Electrocatalytic nitrate reduction tests
The electrochemical measurements were carried out using CHI 660E electrochemical working station in an H-typed cell separated by a cation exchange membrane (Nafion 117). A three-electrode system was employed for all the tests. The carbon paper loaded with $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts was used as the working electrode. Ag/AgCl electrode and Pt foil were used as the reference and counter electrodes, respectively. $25\mathrm{\mL}0.1\mathrm{\m}$ PBS as the electrolyte was added to both the cathode and anode compartments. Then NaNO3 was added into the cathode compartment to acquire an initial $N O_{3}^{-}{-}N$ concentration of $50~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ . After degassed using Argon bubbling to remove the dissolved N2 and O2, the ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ tests were conducted with a potentiostatic mode for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at different potentials. During the reaction, $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous solution was sampled for analyzing the concentration of $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ -N, $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ -N and $\mathsf{N H}_{3}–\mathsf{N}$ .
The conversion rate of $N O_{3}^{-}{-}N$ , the product selectivity and faradaic efficiency of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ were calculated as follows to evaluate the performance of the catalysts.
Conversion rate $=\big(\mathsf{C}_{0}–\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}\big)/\mathsf{C}_{0}\ \times\ 100\%(1)$
where the ${\sf C}_{0}$ and $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}$ are the concentration of $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ -N before and after electrocatalytic tests. $C_{\mathrm{p}}$ is the concentration of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}–\mathsf{N}$ or ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ -N. F is the Faradaic constant $(96485C~\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ ), $\mathsf{V}$ is the volume (L) of the electrolyte solution $\mathrm{:25~mL}$ ), ${\mathsf{C}}_{\mathrm{NH3}}$ and $\mathsf{M}_{\mathrm{NH3}}$ is the mass concentration and molar mass of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ , $\mathsf{Q}$ is the total charge (C) passing through the electrode.
## 2.5. In situ electrochemical tests
The DEMS test was carried out using a differential electrochemical mass spectrometer (QAS 100, Linglu Instruments Co. Ltd., China). 0.1 M PBS with $50\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{NO}_{3^{-}}^{-}$ N was used as the electrolyte. The catalyst ink dropped onto carbon paper was served as the working electrode. After purging the Ar gas for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the DEMS test was conducted. The mass signals were obtained when linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) test was employed from 0 to $-1.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ with a scan rate of $2{\mathrm{~mV}}/{\mathsf{s}}$ . The differential mass signals were collected for three times by repeating the LSV test at the same conditions to avoid accidental errors during DEMS measurements.
In situ SEIRAS test was conducted on INVENIO R spectrometer (Bruker) which was equipped with an in situ electrochemical IR cell. Gold thin film was deposited on a Si prism via a chemical process according to the literature [34,35]. The reflecting plane of the Si prism was polished with ${\sf A l}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ slurries and cleaned with water and ethanol. Then it was soaked in piranha solution (A mixture of 6 mL $98\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}0_{4}$ and 2 $\mathrm{nL}30\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ) for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . The reflecting plane was treated with $40\%$ $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ for $90~s$ , followed by immersion in a mixture of $4~\mathrm{mL}$ Au plating solution and $34~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $50\%$ HF solution at $55^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 min. After that, the catalyst ink $(100\upmu\mathrm{L})$ as described above was then dropped onto the Au film and dried at $40~^{\circ}C$ . The gold film loaded with the catalyst was used as working electrode for the in situ IR test. 0.1 M PBS with 50 ppm $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ -N was used as the electrolyte. The in situ IR spectra were obtained during LSV test from 0 to $-1.5{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ with a scan rate of $2~\mathrm{mV}/\varsigma$ in an Ar atmosphere. Each reflectance spectrum was collected with a time resolution of $30~s~\$ at a spectral resolution of $4~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Then, all of the spectra were transformed into absorbance spectra according to $\mathsf{A}=-\log(\mathsf{R}/\mathsf{R}_{0})$ , where R is the reflectance at the sample potential and ${\tt R}_{0}$ is the reflectance at the reference potential.
## 2.6. DFT calculations
All DFT calculations were performed with the ‘‘Vienna ab initio simulation package” (VASP 5.4.1). The core electrons were treated with the projector-augmented wave (PAW) pseudopotentials (with 400 eV energy cutoff). The exchange–correlation potential was treated using the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) of Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE). K points in the Brillouin zone were set to $3\times3\times1$ within the Monkhorst–Pack grid for both structural optimization and electronic structural calculations. The FCC structures for $\mathrm{Cu}_{72}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{62}\mathrm{Ni}_{10}$ , $\mathsf{C u}_{36}\mathsf{N i}_{36}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{72}$ were generated and tested with the VASP calculations. The (111) surface was cleaved and a $2\times2\times2$ supercell was constructed. The vacuum slab was set to $15.0\mathsf{A}$ to avoid interaction between neighboring supercells. Geometry relaxation was carried out until the residual force was no larger than $0.03\ \mathrm{eV}/\mathsf{A}.$ .
The ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ reduction reaction pathway on different catalysts surfaces was summarized as the following reactions:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{100s-11}}\\ &{\mathrm{1010s_{1}+\cdots-110N0s i;}}\\ &{\mathrm{1100s_{1}+\cdots-110N0s i;}}\\ &{\mathrm{11010s_{2}+\cdots-110s i;}}\\ &{\mathrm{200si,}}\\ &{\mathrm{100,}}\\ &{\mathrm{100si,}\ \mathrm{111}}\\ &{\mathrm{1100si,}}\\ &{\mathrm{100,}}\\ &{\mathrm{10010~si,}\ \mathrm{111}}\\ &{\mathrm{2010si,}}\\ &{\mathrm{20100si,}}\\ &{\mathrm{20100~si,}\ \mathrm{1010si,}}\\ &{\mathrm{20100si,}}\\ &{\mathrm{12000si,}\ \mathrm{1010,}}\\ &{\mathrm{20100si,}\ \mathrm{1010,}}\\ &{\mathrm{20100si,}\ \mathrm{1010,}}\\ &{\mathrm{80.161,}}\end{array}
$$
where $*$ represents the adsorption site. The reaction free energy can be calculated with the equation below:
$$
\Delta G=\Delta\mathrm{EDFT}+\Delta\mathrm{EZPE}-\mathrm{T}\Delta\mathrm{S}(14)
$$
where $\Delta\mathbb{E}$ is the reaction energy difference between reactants and products of each reaction; DEZPE andDS is the change in zero point energy and entropy for each reaction. The zero point energy and entropy of adsorbates and free molecules were obtained from the vibrational frequency calculations.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Microstructure and chemical composition
The $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts were prepared by a solvothermalpyrolysis method. Firstly, a series of bimetallic CuNi-MOFs were synthesized using ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ as metal nodes and 1, 3, 5- benzenetricarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ as organic ligand with the assistance of polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP). XRD patterns show that all of the as-synthesized bimetallic MOFs have similar crystal structure with Cu-BTC, indicating that introducing $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to a certain content (less than $50\%$ ) does not change the framework structure (Fig. S1). When the molar ratio of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ is increased to 1:1, the crystalline phase will not be pure with a new peak appearing at $7.5^{\circ}$ . SEM images show that both Cu-MOF and CuNi-MOF are regular octahedrons (Fig. S2). Elemental mapping results exhibit that Cu, Ni, C, O are homogeneously distributed in the frameworks, further demonstrating the successful fabrication of bimetallic MOFs (Fig. S3). Besides, a small amount $3.3\%$ atomic percentage) of N is still maintained in MOF structure, which might be attributed to the adsorbed and coordinated PVP molecules on the particle surface or DMF used as solvent in the synthesis of MOFs.
Thermal treatment was conducted under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere to in situ transform CuNi-MOFs into $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts. During the pyrolysis process, the BTC ligand serves as both the carbon source and reactant, while the metal ions serves as the precursors of alloy to form the final carbon encapsulated structure. PVP molecules also provide carbon/nitrogen source and facilitate the formation of CuNi alloy and prevent the aggregation of the alloy nanoparticles, without which isolated Cu and Ni particles are obtained under the same synthetic conditions (Fig. S4). The crystalline phase of the as-prepared catalysts was characterized by XRD (Fig. 1a). The XRD patterns of $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ and $\mathsf{N i/N C}$ show three characteristic peaks at $43.3^{\circ}$ , $50.4^{\circ}$ , $74.1^{\circ}$ and $44.5^{\circ}$ , $51.8^{\circ}$ , $76.4^{\circ}$ , separately, which can be well indexed to the (111), (200), (220) facets of facecentered cubic Cu (JCPDS no. 04-0836) and Ni (JCPDS no. 04- 0850), respectively [36,37]. Similar diffraction peaks located between that of Cu and Ni are obtained in the $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ bimetallic catalysts, indicating the formation of CuNi polycrystalline solid solutions. With the increment of Ni loading, the main peaks all shift to higher angles, revealing that the inclusion of Ni to form the bimetallic alloys compresses the crystal lattice. The compressive lattice strain increases from $0.31\%$ in $\mathsf{C u N i/N C-91}$ to $1.38\%$ in CuNi/NC-11 (Table S1). Weak $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ diffraction peak at $36.4^{\circ}$ is detected in Cu-rich samples, which should be attributed to the oxidation of surface Cu atoms upon exposing to air. The diffraction peaks of carbon are hardly visible compared to the high intensity of the metallic components. Thus, Raman spectra were further utilized to verify the presence of carbon matrix. As shown in Fig. S5, two characteristic peaks located at ${\sim}1340$ and $\sim1590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to D and G bands of the carbon matrix [38]. The relative content of Cu and Ni in $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts was measured with XRF and the exact molar ratio was found to be 11.38, 6.08, 3.46, 1.51 when the feeding ratio was 9:1, 5:1, 3:1, 1:1, respectively (Table S2).
Nitrogen adsorption–desorption measurements were conducted to investigate the pore structure of $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts. $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}{\mathrm{-}}51$ possesses a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $83.3\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is a little lower than that of $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ $(106.8~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , Fig. 1b and Fig. S6). The nitrogen sorption isotherm is type IV with a hysteresis loop that is characteristic of mesoporous materials. The pore size distribution illustrates that $\mathrm{CuNi}/$ NC-51 catalyst exhibit abundant mesopores with an average size of 3.9 and $15.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The high BET surface area and mesoporous structure can benefit the mass transfer of electrolyte and reactants during the electrocatalytic reactions. XPS was used to determine the chemical composition and elemental states of $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts as shown in Fig. 1c-f and Fig. S7. Monometallic $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ displays the $\mathtt{C u2p}$ , C1s, N1s and O1s peaks. The high-resolution $\mathtt{C u2p}$ spectrum displays two typical bands at 932.9 and $952.8\mathrm{eV},$ , assigned to the $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathsf{C u}^{0}/\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ , respectively [39]. These bands shift to 933.0 and $952.8{\mathrm{~eV}}$ in CuNi/NC-51 probably resulting from the interaction between Cu and Ni in the alloy. The Ni 2p spectrum of CuNi/NC-51 reveals that there exist two nickel species, where the peaks located at 853.0 and 870.1 eV show the presence of ${\mathsf{N i}}^{0}$ . The bands at 854.7 and $871.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ confirm the presence of surface $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ due to the partial oxidation of metallic Ni in the air, which have been observed in other CuNi alloy catalysts [36,40,41]. The peaks at 859.1 and $875.7\mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to the satellite peaks of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ . The spectra of C and N can give the bonding information of the carbon matrix in the samples. The C1s spectrum exhibits four peaks with binding energies of 284.8, 285.9, 287.9 and $290.3{\mathrm{~eV}}$ which are assigned to CAC, CAN/CAO, $scriptstyle0=C-H$ $\scriptstyle0=\mathbf{C}-0$ , respectively [42]. The N1s spectrum reveals the presence of four types of N species: pyridinic N at $398.7\mathrm{eV}$ , pyrrolic N at $400.3\mathrm{eV}$ , graphitic N at $401.5\mathrm{eV}$ and oxidized N at $403.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ [43,44]. Pyridinic and Pyrrolic N are the dominant nitrogen species in $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts. Pyridinic N in the graphene layer may form $\mathsf{C u/N i{-}N}$ bonds with the surface atoms of CuNi alloy and facilitate charge transfer for nitrate reduction reaction, promoting the reaction activity [45– 47]. The surface composition is extracted from XPS results (Table S3). The surface atomic ratios of Cu and Ni in the samples of CuNi/NC-91, CuNi/NC-51, CuNi/NC-31 and CuNi/NC-11 are 8.33, 5.50, 2.83 and 1.16 from XPS analysis, while the ratios are 11.38, 6.08, 3.46, 1.51 in the bulk from XRF measurements. The trend of Cu-Ni ratio in different samples is consistent on the surface and in the bulk.
The CuNi/NC catalysts show the octahedral morphology that inherits from the pristine CuNi-MOFs as shown in Fig. 2 and Fig. S8. The CuNi alloy particles are embedded in carbon network derived from the decomposition of the organic ligands. A large amount of CuNi alloy particles are embedded in the octahedral carbon martrix. SEM images also reveal that there exists rich macropores on the surface of CuNi/NC octahedral particles and the inner part is composed of porous carbon network that can be seen from a broken particle. These results are consistent with the BET measurements and demonstrate that CuNi/NC materials exhibit hierarchically porous structure. The microstructure of the catalyst was further investigated with TEM. Small particles with size of about $20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and some large particles coexist in the carbon network (Fig. 2c). The $2.1\mathrm{~\AA~}$ spacing observed in the high-resolution TEM image of $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ is assigned to the (1 1 1) facet of CuNi alloy particles, which is in line with XRD measurement (Fig. 2d and Fig. S9). It also clearly reveals that the CuNi alloy particles are encapsulated by graphitic carbon layer with a lattice spacing of $3.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ [40]. HAADF-STEM and elemental mapping results confirm the uniform distribution of Cu, Ni, C, N in the bimetallic sample, as shown in Fig. 2e. The above spectroscopic and microscopic characterizations collectively demonstrate that the copper-nickel alloys encapsulated in nitrogen-doped graphene shell could be prepared through the direct high temperature pyrolysis of bimetallic MOF precursors under inert atmosphere. The alloy nanoparticles are supported on the conductive carbon matrix with hierarchically porous structure, which is favorable for the charge and mass transfer during the electrocatalytic reaction.
## 3.2. Performance and selectivity for electrocatalytic $N O_{3}R R$ to ammonia
The electrocatalytic ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ performance of $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts was measured in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ PBS with 50 ppm nitrate-N using a standard three-electrode configuration. To determine the potential range of reduction reaction, LSV tests were firstly performed in the electrolytes with or without $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ (Fig. 3a). Without the presence of nitrate, the reduction peak of LSV plot arises from hydrogen evolution reaction. After the addition of $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ the LSV curve of $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ showed obvious increase of current density, indicating it is active for $\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{R R}.$ Compared to $\mathsf{C u/N C,C u N i/N C}$ delivers a significant increase of current density, suggesting enhanced ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ performance (Fig. S10). Fig. 3b-d shows the $\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{R R}$ performance of the catalysts with different Cu-Ni molar ratios in $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ potentiostatic electrolysis at $-1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ versus $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ . The ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ conversion rate shows a slight decrease when small amount of nickel is incorporated $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathsf{C u{-}N i}$ molar ratio is lower than 5:1). The ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ conversion rate is higher than $80\%$ in the cases of $\mathrm{Cu{-}N C,\thinspace C u N i/N C{-}91}$ and $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}{-}51$ . With the nickel content increasing further, the $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ conversion rate decreases significantly. The ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ selectivity of CuNi/NC catalysts display volcano shape with the maximum of $94.4\%$ with the $\mathsf{C u{\mathrm{-}}N i}$ ratio of 5:1. The selectivity is always above $90\%$ when $\mathsf{C u{\mathrm{-}}N i}$ ratio is between 9:1 and 1:1. For comparison, the $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ selectivity is only $60.8\%$ for $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ and $44.3\%$ for Ni/NC. The FE of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ also gradually increases to $79.6\%$ with the $\mathsf{C u{\mathrm{-}}N i}$ ratio of 5:1 and then decreases to less than $10\%$ for $\mathsf{N i/N C}$ catalyst. These results demonstrate that the introduction of appropriate nickel into copper can greatly boost the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ selectivity. Meanwhile, the production of nitrite is drastically inhibited for the $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ catalysts compared with bare $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ The selectivity of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ is only $4.6\%$ for $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}{\boldsymbol{-}}51$ and $2.4\%$ for $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}{-31}$ , distinctly lower than $35.0\%$ corresponding to $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ . To verifying the nitrogen source in the synthesized ammonia, $^{15}\mathrm{N}$ labeling experiment was conducted. As shown in Fig. S11, the $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectrum utilizing $^{14}\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ as the reactant exhibits typical triple peaks of $^{14}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{4}^{+}$ while the spectrum using $^{15}\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ as the reactant exhibits the characteristic double peaks of $^{15}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{4}^{+}$ and no peaks of $^{14}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{4}^{+}$ are present, illustrating the ammonia is coming from nitrate other than the N element in the catalysts.
On the basis of the above results, the best comprehensive performance was achieved on the $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}{\boldsymbol{-}}51$ catalyst. Thus, we choose this catalyst for further investigation. The influence of cathode potential on the electrocatalytic performance was evaluated in terms of ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ conversion rate, ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ selectivity and faradaic efficiency (Fig. 4a-b). With the potential proceeding negatively, $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}{\boldsymbol{-}}51$ delivers a gradual increase of ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ conversion and eventually attains $83.0\%$ at $-1.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ Fig. 4b illustrates that the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ selectivity increases from $85.7\%$ at $-0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ to $94.4\%$ at $-1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ and then decreases to $88.0\%$ at $-1.2{\mathrm{~V}}.$ The faradaic efficiency (FE) exhibits a gradual increase from $75.5\%$ at $-0.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ to $83.1\%$ at $-0.9\mathrm{V}.$ However, the FE decreases with the potential moving more negatively because the hydrogen evolution becomes more intensified at more negative potentials. At $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ the evolution of $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ during the reaction was monitored (Fig. 4c). With the prolonging of reaction time, the $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ concentration keeps decreasing while the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ concentration increases continuously, suggesting that ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ has been converted to $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ . More importantly, the $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ concentration is kept at a low level throughout the ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ process with the maximum value of $3.4~\mathsf{p p m}$ , indicating the suppressing of the production of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ . Nitrite is more toxic than nitrate and undesirable in ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ process. Nevertheless, $\mathsf{N O}_{2}^{-}$ is frequently detected as an intermediate or byproduct on copper-based cathodes [48,49]. The above results demonstrate that the doping of nickel into copper can boost the ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ selectivity and significantly attenuate the release of nitrite. As shown in Fig. 4d and Fig. S12, the $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ selectivity, FE and ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ removal efficiency display no decay in five cycles of reaction, proving excellent stability of the catalyst. Notably, the electrocatalytic ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ performance of $\mathsf{C u N i/N C}$ in terms of NH3 selectivity and FE is superior to other reported typical catalysts (Table S4).
## 3.3. Reaction mechanism and kinetics for electrocatalytic $N O{_3R R}$
Electrochemical online DEMS and in situ SEIRAS spectroscopy were used to follow the possible reaction intermediates and study the reaction pathway of $\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{R R}.$ Fig. 5a and Fig. S13 displays the DEMS results during the LSV test from 0 to $-1.2\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ for three circles. The strongest signal with $m/z$ of 17 is attributed to the end product of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ and the weak signal with $m/z$ of 30 could be assigned to the intermediate of NO. Besides, the weak peaks with $m/z$ of 33 and 46 are also detected, which could be attributed to $\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{O H}$ and $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ . The signals shown in Fig. S13 with $m/z$ of 14, 15, 16 might be assigned to the fragments of $\mathsf{N H}_{3}$ and the signal of $m/z$ 31 might be from HNO intermediate [50]. Furthermore, in situ SEIRAS measurement was conducted to monitor the intermediates on the working electrode. Fig. 5b shows potentialdependent absorbance spectra with respect to the reference potential (0 V). The bands at 1588, 1252 and $1454~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are corresponding to the characteristic frequencies of $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ molecularly adsorbed ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ respectively [51]. Then the bands are gradually increasing with the potential moving to more negatively side, indicating the formation of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ . The band at $1325~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be assigned to matrix $\mathsf{N O}^{-}$ reduced from NO intermediate [52]. Moreover, a shoulder peak located at $1181~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared, indicating the existence of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{O H}$ intermediate [26]. The in situ SEIRAS results are consistent with the online DEMS analysis and the reaction pathway can be deduced (The detailed reaction paths are listed in Section 2.6).
To gain deeper insight of the reaction mechanism, we performed DFT calculations for CuNi alloys with varying compositions. Compared to monometallic catalyst, both electronic structure and geometric structure could be modified by forming alloys with another metal [53]. For transition metals, $d$ -band center is an important descriptor for the catalytic performance. The binding strength of intermediates is strongly associated with the position of $d$ -band [54,55]. Four model catalysts with different compositions are constructed, i.e. $\mathrm{Cu}_{72}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{62}\mathrm{Ni}_{10}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}_{36}\mathrm{Ni}_{36}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{72}$ (Fig. S14). Fig. S15 shows the calculated density of states and $d$ - band center of these catalysts. As Ni content increases, the $d.$ - band center shifts upwards toward the Fermi level by 0.31, 0.86 and $1.05\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathsf{C u}_{62}\mathsf{N i}_{10},$ $\mathrm{Cu}_{36}\mathrm{Ni}_{36}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{72}$ , respectively, which leads to the enhanced binding strength of intermediates of ${\tt N O}_{3}{\tt R R}$ on the catalyst surface. The enhanced binding inhibits the desorption of intermediates and facilitates their further hydrogenation to produce ammonia.
